WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.025 --> 00:00:01.840
INTRO:
Over the past decade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:07.680
the reputation of oil companies have only&nbsp;
gotten worse and worse. Not only do they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:12.800
contribute to climate change, but they’re also&nbsp;
actively draining Earth’s natural resources.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:18.000
So, it’s not surprising that many have been&nbsp;
supporting renewable forms of energy as much as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.120
possible from buying electric cars to installing&nbsp;
solar panels. Despite their efforts though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:29.440
our society is still very much dependent on oil&nbsp;
whether its to run factories or to fuel planes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:33.840
And, it’s no secret that oil prices have&nbsp;
skyrocketed over the past several months.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:41.040
This has caused gas prices to reach nearly $5 per&nbsp;
gallon across the US. While this is terrible for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:45.440
everyday people who are just trying to make&nbsp;
ends meet, this has been a blessing for oil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:51.360
companies. Exxon’s net profits, for example, have&nbsp;
jumped from all time lows to multi year highs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:56.080
and this has sent the stock soaring. From&nbsp;
the pandemic bottom, Exxon has skyrocketed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:03.440
250% which correlates to a market cap gain of&nbsp;
$314 billion. This has served oil investors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:11.840
like Warren Buffett extremely well, but this is&nbsp;
also a terrible economic sign and here’s why.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:14.400
WHY IS OIL MOONING?:&nbsp;

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.800
Before we jump into why this is terrible for&nbsp;
the economy, we should first take a look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:24.240
why oil prices are skyrocketing in the first&nbsp;
place. At first glance, it’s easy to just blame&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:29.440
higher oil prices on sky high inflation,&nbsp;
but really, it’s not as a simple as that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:34.880
There’s a multitude of reasons behind this rise&nbsp;
starting with an unprecedented surge in demand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:39.360
Most people have been cooped up inside their&nbsp;
homes eversince the pandemic started. And now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:44.480
that covid cases are finally subsiding, people&nbsp;
are more excited than ever to leave their city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:49.360
state, or even country. Whether its a simple&nbsp;
vacation or a trip to meet family in different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:54.160
states or countries, people are willing to&nbsp;
spend significant amounts to get out. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:59.840
given that aviation industry accounts for 7.8%&nbsp;
of global oil consumption, it’s not surprising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:04.880
that this V shaped recovery in airline demand has&nbsp;
contributed to a V shaped recovery in oil prices.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:09.840
But, the contribution of airlines is nothing&nbsp;
in comparison to the contribution of road based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:15.440
vehicles which has also boomed. We’re seeing more&nbsp;
people go to stores, malls, theathers, concerts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:20.880
music festivals, road trips, etc. But, even more&nbsp;
importantly, most companies have started to call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:24.960
back their remote employees which has led to&nbsp;
one hour commutes becoming the norm once again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:26.321
for many. And with leaders like Elon Musk waging&nbsp;
a full on war against remote work, it’s just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.321 --> 00:02:27.200
matter of time until more companies call back all&nbsp;
of their employees. And with passenger cars and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:32.480
freight vehicles accounting for 49.3% of global&nbsp;
oil consumption, it’s no wonder that a return to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:38.000
normalcy has increased demand substantially. Aside&nbsp;
from increasing demand, we’ve also been facing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:43.040
major geopolitical conflict which is of course the&nbsp;
Russia Ukraine war. Much of the world sides with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:47.840
Ukraine and to show their allegience, they’ve been&nbsp;
boycotting Russian goods. The US, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:53.520
has shut off imports of Russian oil, natural gas,&nbsp;
and coal. Russia only accounts for about 10% of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:58.000
the global oil supply, but this itself makes them&nbsp;
one of the top three oil producers in the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:03.680
only beaten out by the US and Saudi Arabia. So,&nbsp;
cutting of Russian oil not only reduces supply,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:08.160
but it also gives other oil producers more&nbsp;
pricing power. One of the main reasons that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:13.360
oil got so cheap in during the pandemic was&nbsp;
actually thanks to Russia. You see, Russia and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:17.653
Saudi Arabia were having an oil war during the&nbsp;
pandemic which pumped up oil production to 30&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.653 --> 00:03:23.760
year highs while demand was at all time lows. This&nbsp;
even led to oil prices going negative momentarily.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:28.960
But now, we no longer have Russia to play these&nbsp;
crazy commodity games. And speaking of commodity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:34.160
games, the people who are most notorious for such&nbsp;
games are no doubt Wall Street. A solid portion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:38.720
of Wall Street has been speculating for oil&nbsp;
prices to go up eversince the pandemic started,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:44.800
but wait a minute, how does Wall Street buying oil&nbsp;
futures have anything to do oil prices themselves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:49.280
After all, it’s not like hedge funds are directly&nbsp;
buying barrels of oil. They’re basically just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:54.720
betting on whether oil will go up or down in the&nbsp;
coming months and years. Well, here’s the thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:59.600
when Wall Street has optimistic expectations for&nbsp;
oil prices, oil producers are less wiling to sell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:03.600
their oil because theoretically, according to Wall&nbsp;
Street, they should be able to sell their oil for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:09.360
a higher price in the future. On the flipside, oil&nbsp;
consumers get more desperate to buy oil right now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:14.160
because, according to Wall Street, it’s only gonna&nbsp;
get more expensive. So, while Wall Street doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:18.160
directly contribute to the supply and demand of&nbsp;
oil, they play quite a significant psychological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:23.280
role. And when you combine historic inflation, a&nbsp;
v shaped recovery in demand, the Russian boycott,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:27.840
and Wall Street speculation, it’s no wonder&nbsp;
why oil prices are going to the moon.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:31.840
ECONOMIC HARDSHIP:&nbsp;

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:36.400
Now that we have a better understanding as to why&nbsp;
oil prices are skyrocketing in the first place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:41.200
we can move onto its impact on the economy. In&nbsp;
terms of microeconomics, I don’t even think I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:46.720
need to explain anything. For most of us, gas is&nbsp;
an essential. You need it go to work, shop for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:51.840
groceries, drop kids off at school, etc. Even if&nbsp;
you rely on public transportation like busses and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:56.800
subways, it’s only a matter of time until ticket&nbsp;
prices and annual passes increase accordingly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:01.280
So, sooner or later, we’ll all be forced to&nbsp;
allocate more money towards transportation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:06.480
For many, this increase is enough to break&nbsp;
the bank, especially for those living in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:10.880
high cost states like California. But, even&nbsp;
if you’re able to afford the increased cost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:14.640
you’re remaining budget will no doubt decrease&nbsp;
which means less discretionary spending.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:20.240
And given that consumer spending is directly&nbsp;
linked to GDP, this is not the best of signs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:24.320
To make things worse, its not just everyday&nbsp;
people who are feeling the weight of increasing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:29.120
oil prices either. Companies are very much&nbsp;
feeling the impact as well. Now of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:33.760
there are companies that aren’t dependent on&nbsp;
a physical product like Facebook or Google.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:37.920
But, most companies do rely on shipping in one&nbsp;
form or another whether we’re talking about Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:43.680
and Amazon or Nvidia and Tesla. And as oil prices&nbsp;
boom, so does the transportation expenses for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:49.280
these companies. Also, many of these companies&nbsp;
already run on extremely thin profit margins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:54.640
so increased logistical expenses alone are enough&nbsp;
to push them into unprofitability. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:58.720
even Amazon who has one of the most established&nbsp;
and efficient logistical channels in the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:04.240
is feeling the pain. Last quarter, Amazon’s retail&nbsp;
busines was unprofitable both in North America and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:09.280
internationally. Usually, this wouldn’t be that&nbsp;
big of a problem as companies can just pass on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:14.480
the increased cost to customers. But, given that&nbsp;
people already have less discretionary income,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:19.440
they’re more sensitive to price increases. In&nbsp;
fact, one survey found that 1 in 3 prime members&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:22.880
were considering cancelling their subscriptions&nbsp;
due to the price hikes at the beginning of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:27.360
year. So, many companies are stuck in this&nbsp;
situation where they have increasing expenses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:32.240
but stagnating or even declining revenue. This&nbsp;
problem is made even worse when you consider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:36.080
that companies won’t be able to achieve the&nbsp;
same economies of scale with less revenue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:42.080
So, their cost per dollar of revenue just becomes&nbsp;
even worse. And in such circumstances, there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:47.600
only one obvious option and that’s layoffs. We’re&nbsp;
already seeing a slew of signs that companies are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:51.920
getting desperate to hold up revenue. For&nbsp;
example, we’re hearing rumors that Amazon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:57.680
might have another Prime day in the Fall. And the&nbsp;
timing of this is especially concerning. The fall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:02.640
is usually the best time for companies thanks&nbsp;
to the holiday season. So if Amazon is already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:07.760
trying to boost numbers for this fall, how&nbsp;
bad will demand be in q1 and q2 of next year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:10.400
Clearly, we’re already starting to see the&nbsp;
first signs of decreasing demand appear. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:15.840
if oil prices continue to stay at historically&nbsp;
high levels, things will likely only get worse.

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:19.360
RECESSION INDICATOR:&nbsp;

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:23.680
As companies’ earnings slide and people spend&nbsp;
less money, we’ll see the rest of the economy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:28.720
follow a downward trend as well. Here’s the&nbsp;
thing, when companies earnings are declining,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:33.600
who wants to be investing in companies. Now&nbsp;
of course, some of you contrarians out there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:37.840
are saying that this is the best time to buy&nbsp;
fundamentally strong companies for the long term.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:43.440
And, you may very well be right, but the mob is&nbsp;
not on your side. If they were, the stock market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:48.480
wouldn’t be going down in the first place. So,&nbsp;
this means that we’re not only facing decreasing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:53.680
consumer spending, but we’re also facing&nbsp;
decreasing investing. Usually, in such scenarios,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:57.520
the government would pump up spending, but&nbsp;
they’re also pulling back substantially to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:03.760
fight high inflation. So, consumption, investment,&nbsp;
and government spending are all going down meaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:08.960
that our GDP is basically screwed atleast in the&nbsp;
short to medium term. And given that a recession&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:13.680
is defined by two successive quarters of GDP&nbsp;
decline, chances are pretty good that we enter a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:18.320
recession. Skyrocketing oil prices have actually&nbsp;
been extremely good at predicting recessions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:23.440
historically. Not every recession is characterized&nbsp;
by high oil prices, but everytime oil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:29.920
prices are high, we have a recession, and this has&nbsp;
been true since the 1940s. Between 1946 and 1948,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:36.320
we saw oil prices nearly double. And shortly&nbsp;
after we saw a recession between 1948 and 1949.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:41.920
Similarly, between 1973 and 1974, oil prices&nbsp;
nearly tripled, and we once again saw a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:49.200
recession. We saw the same thing between 1979&nbsp;
and 1980, and 1990 and 1991, and 1998 and 2000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:54.880
and 2007 and 2008. In fact, I don’t think there’s&nbsp;
a single time in this 70 year graph where oil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.880 --> 00:09:00.640
prices spiked and there wasn’t a recession. Now&nbsp;
of course, correlation does not mean causation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:04.000
but considering all of the reasoning that we’ve&nbsp;
already discussed throughout this video, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:08.560
doesn’t seem like this is just a coincidence&nbsp;
either. To make things worse, the most recent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:14.320
spike is more violent than we’ve ever seen with&nbsp;
oil rocketing from $20 to $110 within a matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:21.840
of two years. So, based on history,&nbsp;
things aren’t looking that bright.

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:24.400
THE LIGHT AT THE END:
While we may very well be in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:29.680
for a painful recession and rising unemployment,&nbsp;
there are some brightspots to look forward to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:34.000
Not all recessions are accompanied by a stock&nbsp;
market crash, but given how the market has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:38.240
performing so far, it definitely looks like&nbsp;
this recession is being accompanied by one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:44.720
On average, indices typically peak 7 months before&nbsp;
a recession and the bottom 4 months before the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:49.360
of a recession. Given that the S&amp;P 500 peaked&nbsp;
in December, that would mean that the recession&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:50.583
is starting right now. We don’t know when it’ll&nbsp;
end but historically, right now and the coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.583 --> 00:09:51.360
months would be the best time to buy into indices&nbsp;
and fundamentally strong companies. As for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:56.160
housing market, I don’t think there’s going to be&nbsp;
a full on crash like 2008 or anything, but a cool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.160 --> 00:10:00.720
down is extremely likely and this could help many&nbsp;
people buy their first home without having to bid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:05.520
insane amounts over asking. Aside from providing&nbsp;
a great buying opportunity, recessions generally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:10.480
wipe out the garbage on the market. And there’s&nbsp;
not doubt loads of garbage within the stock market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:15.760
and especially the cryptocurrency market. I mean,&nbsp;
I don’t even think we need to discuss NFTs or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:20.240
all of the dog faced coins on the market. So,&nbsp;
this could be a great time to cleanse the space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:26.240
and start fresh. There may be some non financial&nbsp;
benefits as well. With gas prices being so high,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:29.840
it’s likely that many upper middle class&nbsp;
families will make the jump to EVs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:34.640
and this would be a great boost for the entire&nbsp;
industry. But most importantly, a recession may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:39.200
help cool down the out of control inflation that&nbsp;
we’re currently facing. And that may be just what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:44.720
we need to transition into a new period of growth&nbsp;
and prosperity, but that’s just what I think. Do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:49.600
you think we’re in a recession? Comment that down&nbsp;
below. Also, drop a like if you can’t wait to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:54.160
to the otherside of this recession. And of course,&nbsp;
consider joining our discord community to suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.160 --> 00:10:57.840
future video ideas and consider subscribing&nbsp;
to see more questions logically answered.

